{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbaTERD57oPQw4TqEhyE", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Here's a regex for a calendar date in `YYYY-MM-DD` form:\n\n```regex\n^\\d{4}-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])$\n```\n\n- `\\d{4}` matches a four-digit year.\n- `(0[1-9]|1[0-2])` matches a month from 01 to 12.\n- `(0[1-9]|[12]\\d|3[01])` matches a day from 01 to 31.\n- `^` and `$` make the whole string match. Remove them to find dates inside longer text, and use `\\b` instead if you need word boundaries.\n\nIt matches `2026-10-01` and `1999-12-31`. It rejects `2026-13-01`, `2026-1-1` and `2026-10-32`.\n\n**The regex can't check every date.** It accepts dates that don't exist, like `2026-02-30` and `2026-04-31`, because checking month lengths and leap years in a regex is unreadable. If you need to be sure a date is real, use the regex as a first check and then parse it with a date library. For example, in Python, `datetime.date.fromisoformat(s)` raises an error on invalid dates.\n\nIf you only need the format and not valid ranges, `^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$` is enough."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7294, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7294}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f030ef94-e539-406e-bf33-fea889010c99", "uuid": "2f9bd33d-4c96-4bed-8d33-de1cade95e89", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T13:14:22.961Z", "request_id": "req_011CfbaTE4t5XZFtLkikAVEi"}
